  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nIt will get slightly warmer over the next couple of days, leading to \na pleasant Halloween weekend. Low level ridge will remain parked \nover the Mid-South region as we head into Saturday, keeping the \nweather dry and peaceful. Still could see patchy fog each morning, \nprimarily near the rivers and lakes. \n\nLooking towards the weekend, Halloween itself is likely to be dry \nfor candy hunting, frights and thrills, and costume parties. A \nsubtle shortwave arrives on Sunday, but poor moisture return means \nany rainfall is likely to remain on the lighter side. Euro and the \nAI guidance suite both keep the better rain amounts south of the \nTennessee - Georgia state line, but light rain amounts may still \nfall in East Tennessee. Think the northern extent of the rain \nchances may be overdone, given the dryness we're currently in and \nthe QPF model fields, but I only made minor trims to the PoPs. High \ntemperatures on Sunday are likely to be more muted thanks to the \nwidespread cloud cover.\n\nNext week we will likely see another couple dry fall weather days, \nleaning on the more mild side similar to this past week. Midweek \ninto late week ensemble guidance depicts potential for a southern US \ntrough that will be the next system to watch to fill our growing \nneed of rain.",
